Within Few-NERD, the degeneracy-gating pattern holds at entity-type granularity: 7 of 8 types show positive LP selection gains ($+0.23$ to $+4.73$~pp), while the highest-degeneracy type (Art, 19.8\%) is the sole exception (Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain. Blue circles: cross-dataset aggregates (CoNLL, SciERC, Few-NERD); orange triangles: per-entity-type within Few-NERD. The dashed line at ${\sim}$15\% separates a regime where LP selection improves over greedy (left) from one where it fails (right). Cross-dataset ordering is monotonic; 7/8 Few-NERD entity types below 20\% degeneracy show positive gains. Degeneracy below ${\sim}$15\% appears necessary for LP selection to succeed (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.57$, $p{=}0.066$, $n{=}11$).}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter} visualizes the relationship across both dataset-level and entity-type-level granularity: degeneracy below ${\sim}$15\% is a necessary condition for LP selection gains.

